ENCS 5412 switch — HYPERVISOR bundle
====================================

This directory ships inside the bootstrap VM but is NOT meant to run here.
Copy it to the Proxmox host and install it there.

WHY
---
The bootstrap VM's only job is to push firmware into the Marvell ASIC over
PCIe. It carries no traffic and cannot reach the switch's management IP.

The switch answers on 169.254.1.0 over VLAN 2363 on the X710 backplane NIC —
and that NIC belongs to the hypervisor. So management lives on the host.

    front gi0-gi7 -> Marvell ASIC -> te1/te2 10G -> host X710 -> you
                        ^ PCIe
                        |
                  bootstrap VM (this machine — boot only)

INSTALL
-------
From the Proxmox host, with <vm-ip> being this VM:

    scp -r root@<vm-ip>:/opt/encs-host /root/
    bash /root/encs-host/install.sh

install.sh will:
  * find the X710 backplane port (it does NOT hardcode a name — it is
    enp8s0f1np1 on one box and something else on the next)
  * create VLAN interface sw2363 with 169.254.1.1/16, MTU 9216
    (the VLAN iface CANNOT be called enp8s0f1np1.2363 — 16 chars exceeds the
    15-char IFNAMSIZ limit and ip link rejects it)
  * persist that to /etc/network/interfaces
  * install encs-switch-tui and encs-switch-api to /usr/local/sbin
  * install and enable encs-switch-replay.service
  * verify the switch answers

Then just run:

    encs-switch-tui          press ? for the built-in manual

Ports, VLANs, PoE, link aggregation (Ports view, 'g'), MAC table, statistics
and config save/replay.

UPDATING
--------
These host tools are independent of the disk image — the ISO and qcow2 only
carry the bootstrap VM. Updating them NEVER requires rebuilding anything.

    encs-switch-tui --version         what is installed
    encs-switch-tui --check-update    is there anything newer?
    encs-switch-tui --update          fetch and install it (needs root)

The TUI also checks once at startup and says so in the title bar. Hosts with
no route to github.com see nothing and are not slowed down;
ENCS_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1 skips the check entirely.

--update verifies SHA256SUMS, refuses anything that will not compile, and
backs up what it replaces into /var/backups/encs-switch/<timestamp>/. It
writes only to /usr/local/sbin, /etc/systemd/system and /opt/encs-host —
never to /etc/encs-switch or your network config.

To update by hand instead, re-copy this directory from a newer checkout and
re-run install.sh; it is idempotent. Or just scp encs-switch-tui over the
installed one — it is a single stdlib-only Python script.

CONTENTS
--------
  install.sh                   host installer (run on Proxmox)
  encs-switch-tui              the management UI; also --save/--apply/--status
  encs-switch-api              low-level XML API client for scripting
  encs-switch-replay.service   reapplies config after a cold boot

THE TWO THINGS TO REMEMBER
--------------------------
1. Config is volatile. The ASIC has no flash. VM restarts are fine — they do
   not power-cycle it — but any real power loss wipes the config back to
   VLAN 1 + 2363 with every front port SHUT and PoE off. Save your config
   (TUI: c then w) and let encs-switch-replay put it back.

2. Never restart the bootstrap service, and avoid stopping the VM. The loader
   can only bootstrap an ASIC in WFI; re-running it against a live switch
   wedges it, and the only recovery is PHYSICAL AC REMOVAL — a CIMC power-off
   is not enough. Note that a wedged loader does not mean a dead switch: check
   with 'ping 169.254.1.0', not with the log.

CREDENTIALS
-----------
  Switch (encs-switch-tui / encs-switch-api):  cisco / cisco

That is the ROS firmware default, not a choice this project made — Cisco's own
switch_settings.py hardcodes it for the un-provisioned case. NFVIS would
replace it and keep the new one in ConfD, which we do not run.

Since the ASIC has no flash, a changed switch password is lost on every cold
boot and reverts to cisco/cisco.

Override per-session:  SW_USER=admin SW_PASS=secret encs-switch-tui

The management VLAN (2363, 169.254.1.0/16) is link-local on the internal
backplane and not routable from your LAN unless you bridge or NAT it
deliberately. Given the credentials reset on power loss, keep it that way.
